Here's an explanation of the provided Java API methods for converting a BitSet to an integer:

1. `java.util.BitSet.length()`: This method returns the "logical size" of the BitSet, which is the index of the highest set bit in the BitSet plus one. It serves as a way to determine the number of set bits in the BitSet.

2. `java.util.BitSet.get(index)`: This method returns the value of the bit with the specified index. It serves as a way to check if a specific bit is set in the BitSet.

To convert a BitSet to an integer, you can use the following approach:

```java
public class BitSetToInt {
    public static int bitSetToInt(java.util.BitSet bitSet) {
        int result = 0;
        for (int i = bitSet.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (bitSet.get(i)) {
                result |= 1 << i;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.BitSet bitSet = new java.util.BitSet();
        bitSet.set(0);
        bitSet.set(1);
        bitSet.set(3);
        System.out.println(bitSetToInt(bitSet));
    }
}
```

In this code, we iterate over the BitSet from right to left (i.e., from least significant bit to most significant bit), and for each set bit, we shift 1 to the left by the current bit index and perform a bitwise OR operation with the result. This effectively sets the corresponding bit in the result integer.

