### 1. `replaceAll()`

This method serves to replace all occurrences of a specified pattern in a string with a replacement string. It uses regular expressions to match patterns in the string.

### 2. `quoteReplacement()`

This method serves to quote the replacement string, which means it escapes any special characters in the replacement string, so they are treated as literal characters rather than part of the regular expression. This is useful when using the `replaceAll()` method with a replacement string that contains special characters.

### 3. `Pattern.compile()`

This method serves to compile a regular expression pattern into a `Pattern` object, which can then be used to perform matching and replacement operations. It is used to define the pattern that will be matched in the string.

### 4. `Matcher.matcher()`

This method serves to create a `Matcher` object that can be used to perform matching and replacement operations on a string. It takes a string and a regular expression pattern as input, and returns a `Matcher` object that can be used to match the string against the pattern.

### 5. `Matcher.replaceAll()`

This method serves to replace all occurrences of a specified pattern in a string with a replacement string. It uses the `Matcher` object to perform the replacement, and can be used to replace text in a string while preserving the original case and formatting.

### Example usage:

```java
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello, world! Hello again!";
        String xml = str.replaceAll("Hello", "<b>Hello</b>");
        System.out.println(xml); // Output: <b>Hello</b>, world! <b>Hello</b> again!

        String quotedStr = Matcher.quoteReplacement("Hello");
        xml = str.replaceAll("Hello", quotedStr);
        System.out.println(xml); // Output: <b>Hello</b>, world! <b>Hello</b> again!

        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("Hello");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(str);
        xml = matcher.replaceAll("<b>Hello</b>");
        System.out.println(xml); // Output: <b>Hello</b>, world! <b>Hello</b> again!
    }
}
```

In this example, we use the `replaceAll()` method to replace all occurrences of the string "Hello" with "<b>Hello</b>". We also use the `quoteReplacement()` method to quote the replacement string, and the `Pattern.compile()` and `Matcher.matcher()` methods to compile and match the regular expression pattern.

