- Handles special cases such as division by zero, negative or positive zero, and NaN (Not a Number) inputs.

However, if you want to implement the `pow` method manually without using the `Math.pow` method, you can use the following formula:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(power(2.0, 3.0)); // prints 8.0
        System.out.println(power(2.0, 0.0)); // prints 1.0
        System.out.println(power(2.0, -3.0)); // prints 0.125
        System.out.println(power(0.0, 3.0)); // prints 0.0
    }

    public static double power(double x, double y) {
        if (y == 0) {
            return 1;
        } else if (y < 0) {
            return 1 / power(x, -y);
        } else if (x == 0) {
            return 0;
        } else if (x == 1) {
            return 1;
        } else if (x == -1) {
            return -1;
        } else {
            double result = 1;
            while (y > 0) {
