            probabilities[i] = Math.exp(-lnValues[i]) / Math.exp(-maxLnValue);
        }
        return probabilities;
    }

    public static double findMax(double[] values) {
        double max = values[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
            if (values[i] > max) {
                max = values[i];
            }
        }
        return max;
    }
}
```

Here's how each part of the request serves its purpose:

- `Math.exp(-lnValue)`: This part serves to calculate the probability of each ln value by taking the exponential of the negative ln value. This is based on the definition of probability as the exponential of the negative logarithm.

- `Math.exp(-maxLnValue)`: This part serves to normalize the probabilities so that they sum up to 1. This is done by dividing each probability by the maximum probability, which is the exponential of the maximum ln value.

- `findMax(lnValues)`: This part serves to find the maximum ln value in the array. This is necessary because the probabilities need to be normalized so that they sum up to 1, and this can only be done if we know the maximum value.

