Here's a sample Java code snippet that calculates the area of a convex polygon using the Shoelace formula:

```java
public class PolygonAreaCalculator {

    public static double calculatePolygonArea(double[] xCoordinates, double[] yCoordinates) {
        int n = xCoordinates.length;
        double area = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int j = (i + 1) % n;
            area += xCoordinates[i] * yCoordinates[j];
            area -= xCoordinates[j] * yCoordinates[i];
        }

        area = Math.abs(area) / 2;
        return area;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[] xCoordinates = {0, 0, 3, 4, 0};
        double[] yCoordinates = {0, 4, 4, 0, 0};
        double area = calculatePolygonArea(xCoordinates, yCoordinates);
        System.out.println("The area of the polygon is: " + area);
    }
}
```

This code snippet calculates the area of a convex polygon using the Shoelace formula. The `calculatePolygonArea` method takes two arrays of x and y coordinates as input and returns the area of the polygon. The `main` method demonstrates how to use this method to calculate the area of a sample polygon.

