However, to convert a negative integer into two's complement binary form, we need to use a different approach. Here's a simple Java method that accomplishes this:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(twosComplement(-5));
    }

    public static String twosComplement(int n) {
        // Convert to binary and remove the sign bit
        String binary = Integer.toBinaryString(Math.abs(n));

        // Add 1 to the binary representation
        String binaryWithOne = addOne(binary);

        return binaryWithOne;
    }

    public static String addOne(String binary) {
        // Reverse the binary string
        String reversed = new StringBuilder(binary).reverse().toString();

        // Add 1 to the reversed binary string
        int result = Integer.parseInt(reversed, 2) + 1;

        // Convert the result back to binary and remove the sign bit
        return Integer.toBinaryString(result);
    }
}
```

This code first converts the absolute value of the negative integer to binary, then adds 1 to the binary representation, and finally converts the result back to binary. The result is the two's complement binary form of the original negative integer.

