This method determines if the specified `Class` object represents an interface type. It serves as a simple and straightforward way to check if a class is an interface.

### 2. `java.lang.reflect.Modifier.isInterface()`

This method checks if the specified class or interface is an interface. It serves as a more reflective way to check if a class or interface is an interface, and is often used in conjunction with `java.lang.reflect.Modifier.isAbstract()` to check if a class or interface is both abstract and an interface.

### 3. `java.lang.reflect.Type.isInterface()`

This method checks if the specified type is an interface. It serves as a more general way to check if a type is an interface, and is often used in conjunction with other reflection methods to check the type of an object.

### 4. `java.lang.reflect.Type.isInterfaceClass()`

This method checks if the specified type is an interface class. It serves as a more specific way to check if a type is an interface, and is often used in conjunction with other reflection methods to check the type of an object.

### Example Usage:

```java
import java.lang.reflect.Modifier;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Check if a class is an interface using java.lang.Class
        Class<?> clazz = MyClass.class;
        if (clazz.isInterface()) {
            System.out.println("MyClass is an interface");
        }

        // Check if a class is an interface using java.lang.reflect.Modifier
        if (Modifier.isInterface(clazz.getModifiers())) {
            System.out.println("MyClass is an interface");
