Here's an explanation of the Java API methods for the Stack implementation using an array with a fixed size:

1. `java.lang.String isEmpty()`: This method serves to check if the stack is empty by verifying if the length of the array is 0. It returns true if the stack is empty and false otherwise.

2. `java.lang.String size()`: This method serves to return the number of elements currently stored in the stack. It returns the size of the array, which is fixed and known at the time of creation.

3. `java.lang.String push(Object item)`: This method serves to add an item to the top of the stack. It shifts all existing elements down to make room for the new item, effectively increasing the size of the array.

4. `java.lang.String pop()`: This method serves to remove and return the item at the top of the stack. If the stack is empty, it throws an exception. It decreases the size of the array by one.

5. `java.lang.String peek()`: This method serves to return the item at the top of the stack without removing it. If the stack is empty, it throws an exception. It does not change the size of the array.

6. `java.lang.String elementAt(int index)`: This method serves to return the item at the specified index in the stack. If the index is out of bounds, it throws an exception. It does not change the size of the array.

7. `java.lang.String getCapacity()`: This method serves to return the maximum number of elements that the stack can hold. It returns the fixed capacity of the array.

8. `java.lang.String setCapacity(int capacity)`: This method serves to change the capacity of the stack. It sets the fixed capacity of the array to the specified value. 

Here's a simple implementation of a Stack in Java using an array with a fixed size:

```java
public class FixedSizeStack {
    private Object[] array;
    private int size;
    private int capacity;

    public FixedSizeStack(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.array = new Object[capacity];
        this.size = 0;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public void push(Object item) {
        if (size == capacity) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is full");
        }
        array[size] = item;
        size++;
    }

    public Object pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty");
        }
        Object item = array[size - 1];
        size--;
        return item;
    }

    public Object peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return array[size - 1];
    }

    public Object get(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException();
        }
        return array[index];
    }

    public int getCapacity() {
        return capacity;
    }

    public void setCapacity(int capacity) {
        if (capacity < this.capacity) {
            throw new RuntimeException("New capacity is smaller than current capacity");
        }
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }
}
```

This implementation provides the required methods for a Stack with a fixed size. It uses an array to store the elements and provides methods to push, pop, peek, get, and set the capacity. It also includes checks to prevent index out of bounds and stack overflow/underflow.

