Here's an explanation of the Java API methods for cleaning punctuation by regex:

1. `java.util.regex.Matcher.replaceAll()`: This method serves to replace every subsequence of the input sequence that matches the pattern with the given replacement string. It scans the input sequence looking for matches of the pattern and appends the replacement string to the result.

2. `java.util.regex.Matcher.appendReplacement()`: This method serves to append the replacement string to the result, allowing the replacement string to contain references to captured subsequences. It is used in conjunction with `replaceAll()` to build the replacement string.

3. `java.util.regex.Matcher.appendTail()`: This method serves to append any remaining characters from the input sequence to the result, after all replacements have been made. It is used in conjunction with `replaceAll()` to append any remaining characters.

4. `java.util.regex.Pattern.compile()`: This method serves to compile a regular expression pattern into a `Pattern` object, which can then be used to match strings against the pattern. It is used to define the pattern that will be used for replacement.

5. `java.util.regex.Pattern.quote()`: This method serves to escape special characters in a string so that they are treated as literal characters in a regular expression. It is used to ensure that the replacement string is treated as a literal string, rather than a regular expression.

6. `java.util.regex.Pattern.matcher()`: This method serves to create a `Matcher` object that can be used to match a string against a regular expression pattern. It is used to create a `Matcher` object that can be used with the `replaceAll()` method.

Here's an example of how you can use these methods to clean punctuation by regex:

```java
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "Hello, world! How's it going?";

        // Compile the pattern
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("[^a-zA-Z0-9\\s]");

        // Create a Matcher object
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);

        // Use appendReplacement to build the replacement string
        StringBuffer buffer = new StringBuffer();
        while (matcher.find()) {
            matcher.appendReplacement(buffer, "");
        }
        matcher.appendTail(buffer);

        // Print the cleaned text
        System.out.println(buffer.toString());
    }
}
```

This code will output: "Hello world Hows it going"

