Here's an example of how you can create a fixed thread pool with named daemon threads that will expire after 60 seconds of inactivity in Java:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.ThreadFactory;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class FixedThreadPool {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a fixed thread pool with 5 threads
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5, new ThreadFactory() {
            @Override
            public Thread newThread(Runnable r) {
                Thread thread = new Thread(r);
                thread.setDaemon(true); // Set as daemon thread
                thread.setName("FixedThreadPool-" + thread.getId()); // Set thread name
                return thread;
            }
        });

        // Allow core threads to time out after 60 seconds of inactivity
        executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // Submit tasks to the executor
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            executor.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000); // Simulate some work
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            });
        }

        // Shut down the executor
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

Here's an explanation of the API methods used:

- `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(int nThreads)`: This method creates a fixed thread pool with the specified number of threads. It serves the purpose of creating a thread pool with a fixed number of threads.

- `ThreadFactory`: This is a functional interface that defines a single abstract method `newThread(Runnable r)`. It serves the purpose of customizing the creation of threads in the thread pool.

- `Thread.setDaemon(boolean on)`: This method sets whether the thread is a daemon thread or not. It serves the purpose of setting the thread as a daemon thread, which means it will exit when the main thread exits.

- `Thread.setName(String name)`: This method sets the name of the thread. It serves the purpose of setting a name for the thread, which can be useful for identifying the thread.

- `executor.allowCoreThreadTimeOut(long timeout, TimeUnit unit)`: This method sets the policy governing whether core threads may time out and terminate if no tasks arrive within the keep-alive time. It serves the purpose of allowing core threads to time out after a specified amount of time, which can be useful for preventing the thread pool from holding onto threads indefinitely.

- `executor.shutdown()`: This method shuts down the executor, which serves the purpose of stopping the thread pool from accepting new tasks and allowing existing tasks to complete.

