```java
public class PrimeChecker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 23;
        if (isPrime(number)) {
            System.out.println(number + " is a prime number.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(number + " is not a prime number.");
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        if (number == 2) {
            return true;
        }
        if (number % 2 == 0) {
            return false;
        }
        double sqrt = Math.sqrt(number);
        for (int i = 3; i <= sqrt; i += 2) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

This code snippet uses the `isPrime` method to check if a given number is prime. It first checks if the number is less than or equal to 1, in which case it is not prime. Then it checks if the number is 2, in which case it is prime. Next, it checks if the number is even, in which case it is not prime. Finally, it calculates the square root of the number and checks if it is divisible by any odd number up to the square root. If it is divisible, then the number is not prime. Otherwise, it is prime.

Here's an explanation of the `hasNextInt()` methods in Java's `Scanner` API, along with examples of how to use them:

### `hasNextInt()`

This method checks if the next token in the input can be interpreted as an integer. It does not advance the scanner's position in the input.

```java
// Example usage:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println(scanner.hasNextInt());  // Returns true if the next token is an integer
        scanner.nextLine();  // Consume the newline left-over
        System.out.println(scanner.hasNextInt());  // Returns true if the next token is an integer
    }
}
```

### `hasNextInt(int radix)`

This method checks if the next token in the input can be interpreted as an integer in the specified radix. It does not advance the scanner's position in the input.

```java
// Example usage:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println(scanner.hasNextInt(16));  // Returns true if the next token is an integer in hexadecimal
        scanner.nextLine();  // Consume the newline left-over
        System.out.println(scanner.hasNextInt(16));  // Returns true if the next token is an integer in hexadecimal
    }
}
```

In both cases, the `hasNextInt()` method serves as a safety check to ensure that the next input is an integer before attempting to parse it. This helps prevent `InputMismatchException` from being thrown when trying to parse a non-integer value.

