Here's a sample Java code snippet that demonstrates how to use these API methods to set a session ID as a cookie and retrieve it:

```java
import java.net.CookieStore;
import java.net.HttpCookie;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.util.List;

public class SessionIdToCookie {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a cookie store
        CookieStore cookieStore = new CookieStore();

        // Create a session ID
        String sessionId = "1234567890";

        // Create a cookie with the session ID
        HttpCookie cookie = new HttpCookie("sessionId", sessionId);
        cookie.setMaxAge(3600); // Set the cookie to expire in 1 hour

        // Add the cookie to the cookie store
        cookieStore.add(cookie);

        // Create a request with the cookie
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create("https://example.com"))
                .header("Cookie", cookie.getName() + "=" + cookie.getValue())
                .build();

        // Send the request
        HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        // Get all not-expired cookies from the cookie store
        List<HttpCookie> cookies = cookieStore.getCookies();

        // Print the session ID cookie
        for (HttpCookie c : cookies) {
            if (c.getName().equalsIgnoreCase("sessionId")) {
                System.out.println("Session ID: " + c.getValue());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates a cookie store, sets a session ID as a cookie, adds it to the cookie store, and sends a request with the cookie. It then retrieves all not-expired cookies from the cookie store and prints the session ID cookie.

