Here's how you can check if a thread is alive in Java using the `java.lang.Thread.isAlive()` method:

```java
import java.lang.Thread;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new thread
        Thread thread = new Thread(() -> {
            // Simulate some work
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                System.out.println("Thread is running...");
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
            }
        });

        // Start the thread
        thread.start();

        // Check if the thread is alive
        while (thread.isAlive()) {
            System.out.println("Thread is alive");
            try {
                Thread.sleep(1000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Thread has died");
    }
}
```

The `java.lang.Thread.isAlive()` method serves two purposes:

1.  It tests if the thread has been started and has not yet died. This is useful for checking if a thread is currently running or not.
2.  It can be used to check if a thread has been terminated, i.e., if it has died. This is useful for handling thread-related events, such as when a thread finishes its execution.

Note that `isAlive()` is a non-blocking method, meaning it does not block the current thread if the thread being queried is not alive.

