Here's a Java function that converts an integer into its Roman numeral representation:

```java
public class Main {

    private static final String[] ROMAN_NUMERALS = {"M", "CM", "D", "CD", "C", "XC", "L", "XL", "X", "IX", "V", "IV", "I"};

    private static final int[] VALUES = {1000, 900, 500, 400, 100, 90, 50, 40, 10, 9, 5, 4, 1};

    public static String intToRoman(int num) {
        if (num < 1 || num > 3999) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Number must be between 1 and 3999");
        }

        StringBuilder romanNumeral = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < VALUES.length; i++) {
            while (num >= VALUES[i]) {
                romanNumeral.append(ROMAN_NUMERALS[i]);
                num -= VALUES[i];
            }
        }

        return romanNumeral.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(intToRoman(4));  // Should print IV
        System.out.println(intToRoman(9));  // Should print IX
        System.out.println(intToRoman(13)); // Should print XIII
        System.out.println(intToRoman(44)); // Should print XLIV
        System.out.println(intToRoman(1000)); // Should print M
    }
}
```

