Here's how you can obtain the next Power Of Two of a given number using Java's `Integer.highestOneBit()` method:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 10;
        int nextPowerOfTwo = getNextPowerOfTwo(number);
        System.out.println("The next power of two of " + number + " is " + nextPowerOfTwo);
    }

    public static int getNextPowerOfTwo(int number) {
        return number | (number & -number);
    }
}
```

Explanation of the code:

- `number | (number & -number)` serves two purposes: 
  - `number & -number` clears all bits to the right of the highest one-bit in `number`, effectively giving us the highest one-bit itself. 
  - `number | (number & -number)` then sets the highest one-bit to 1, effectively giving us the next power of two.

Explanation of `Integer.highestOneBit()`:

- `Integer.highestOneBit()` serves the purpose of finding the highest one-bit in a given integer. 
- It returns an int value with at most a single one-bit, in the position of the highest-order ("leftmost") one-bit in the specified int value. 
- If the specified value has no one-bits in its two's complement binary representation, that is, if it is equal to zero, it returns zero.

