To remove auto-focus in Swing, you can use the following Java API methods:

### 1. `requestFocusInWindow()`

This method requests that the component gets the input focus. It serves the purpose of explicitly requesting focus from the window, which can be useful when you want to manually manage focus.

### 2. `requestFocusInWindow(boolean)`

This method is similar to `requestFocusInWindow()`, but it also allows you to specify whether the focus should be requested from the window's focus cycle (i.e., whether the focus should be passed to the next component in the focus cycle). This serves the purpose of controlling the focus flow within the window.

To remove auto-focus, you can call `requestFocusInWindow(false)` to prevent the component from requesting focus from the window. However, this will not remove the focus from the component itself. To remove the focus from the component, you can call `requestFocusInWindow(false)` and then set the component's focusable property to `false`.

Here's an example:

```java
import javax.swing.JComponent;
import javax.swing.JFrame;
import javax.swing.JPanel;

public class RemoveAutoFocus {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Remove AutoFocus");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        JPanel panel = new JPanel();
        panel.setFocusable(true); // Set focusable to true
        panel.requestFocusInWindow(false); // Request focus from the window
        panel.setFocusable(false); // Set focusable to false to remove focus

        frame.add(panel);
        frame.pack();
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `JPanel` is set to be focusable, and then the `requestFocusInWindow(false)` method is called to request focus from the window. Finally, the `setFocusable(false)` method is called to remove the focus from the panel.

