3. `java.util.Arrays.setAll()`: This method serves to populate an array with values generated by a provided function, which in this case is used to calculate the height of each point on the sphere. If the function throws an exception, it is relayed to the caller and the array is left in an indeterminate state.

Here's a simple JavaFX Spherical Height Map example:

```java
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.layout.Pane;
import javafx.scene.paint.Color;
import javafx.scene.shape.DrawMode;
import javafx.scene.shape.Sphere;
import javafx.scene.shape.SpherePoint;
import javafx.scene.shape.SpherePoints;
import javafx.scene.shape.SphereSegment;
import javafx.scene.shape.SphereSegmentBuilder;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

public class SphericalHeightMap extends Application {

    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) {
        Pane root = new Pane();
        Scene scene = new Scene(root, 800, 600);

        // Create a 3D sphere
        Sphere sphere = new Sphere(100);
        sphere.setDrawMode(DrawMode.LINE);

        // Create points on the sphere
        SpherePoints points = new SpherePoints();
        for (int i = 0; i < 360; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 180; j++) {
                double lat = Math.toRadians(j);
                double lon = Math.toRadians(i);
                double height = Math.sin(lat) * 100;
                points.add(new SpherePoint(i, j, height));
            }
        }

        // Create segments on the sphere
        SphereSegmentBuilder builder = new SphereSegmentBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {
            SpherePoint p1 = points.get(i);
            SpherePoint p2 = points.get((i + 1) % points.size());
            builder.add(p1, p2);
        }
        SphereSegment segments = builder.build();

        // Add the sphere and segments to the scene
        root.getChildren().addAll(sphere, segments);

        primaryStage.setScene(scene);
        primaryStage.show();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }
}
```

This code creates a 3D sphere with points and segments on its surface, where the height of each point is calculated using the sine of its latitude. The resulting Spherical Height Map is displayed in a JavaFX window.

