Here's a Java method that recursively searches for enterprise annotations matching the filter:

```java
import java.lang.annotation.Annotation;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.lang.reflect.Modifier;
import java.util.*;

public class EnterpriseAnnotationFinder {

    public static List<Class<?>> findEnterpriseAnnotations(Class<?> clazz, Set<Class<?>> filter) {
        List<Class<?>> matchingAnnotations = new ArrayList<>();

        // Check if the annotation type is in the filter
        if (filter.contains(clazz.annotationType())) {
            matchingAnnotations.add(clazz);
        }

        // Get all methods in the class
        Method[] methods = clazz.getDeclaredMethods();

        // Iterate over each method
        for (Method method : methods) {
            // Check if the method is not synthetic and has the annotation
            if (!Modifier.isSynthetic(method.getModifiers()) && method.isAnnotationPresent(EnterpriseAnnotation.class)) {
                // Recursively search for annotations in the method's return type
                if (method.getReturnType() != void.class) {
                    matchingAnnotations.addAll(findEnterpriseAnnotations(method.getReturnType(), filter));
                }

                // Recursively search for annotations in the method's parameter types
                for (Class<?> paramType : method.getParameterTypes()) {
                    matchingAnnotations.addAll(findEnterpriseAnnotations(paramType, filter));
                }
            }
        }

        return matchingAnnotations;
    }
}
```

Here's an explanation of the Java API methods used in the code:

1. `java.lang.annotation.Annotation.annotationType()`: This method returns the annotation type of the given annotation. It serves as a way to get the type of the annotation, which can be used to check if the annotation is in the filter.

2. `java.lang.reflect.Method.getReturnType()`: This method returns the return type of the given method. It serves as a way to get the type of the value that the method returns, which can be used to recursively search for annotations in the method's return type.

3. `java.lang.reflect.Method.getParameterTypes()`: This method returns an array of the parameter types of the given method. It serves as a way to get the types of the values that the method takes as parameters, which can be used to recursively search for annotations in the method's parameter types.

4. `java.util.Set.contains()`: This method checks if the given element is in the set. It serves as a way to check if the annotation type is in the filter.

5. `java.util.ArrayList.addAll()`: This method adds all elements from the given collection to the end of the list. It serves as a way to accumulate the matching annotations in the list.

