Here's an explanation of the Java API methods for removing cookies from an HttpServletRequest:

1. `java.net.CookieStore.getCookies()`: This method retrieves all not-expired cookies from the cookie store, which serves as a collection of cookies that are currently being stored by the browser. It does not remove cookies, but rather provides a way to access and manage them.

2. `java.net.HttpCookie.setMaxAge()`: This method sets the maximum age of a cookie in seconds, which determines when the cookie will expire. By setting the maximum age to 0, you can effectively remove a cookie from the request.

Here's an example of how you can use these methods to remove a cookie from an HttpServletRequest:

```java
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import java.net.CookieStore;
import java.net.HttpCookie;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;

public class CookieRemover {
    public static void removeCookie(HttpServletRequest request, String cookieName) throws URISyntaxException {
        // Get the cookie store
        CookieStore cookieStore = request.getCookieStore();

        // Get the cookie
        HttpCookie cookie = cookieStore.getCookies().stream()
                .filter(cookie1 -> cookie1.getName().equals(cookieName))
                .findFirst()
                .orElse(null);

        if (cookie != null) {
            // Set the maximum age to 0 to remove the cookie
            cookie.setMaxAge(0);
            cookieStore.remove(cookie);
        }
    }
}
```

This code retrieves the cookie store from the request, finds the cookie with the specified name, and sets its maximum age to 0 to remove it.

