- `java.util.concurrent.Executors.newFixedThreadPool(int nThreads)`: This method creates a thread pool that reuses a fixed number of threads operating off a shared unbounded queue. It serves the request of reusing a thread by allowing multiple tasks to be executed concurrently, and when a thread is available, it will execute the next task in the queue, thus reusing the thread.

Here's an example of how to use it:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a thread pool with 5 threads
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

        // Submit 10 tasks to the executor
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            executor.submit(() -> {
                System.out.println("Task " + i + " is being executed");
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000); // Simulate some work
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                }
                System.out.println("Task " + i + " is completed");
            });
        }

        // Shut down the executor
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

In this example, the `newFixedThreadPool()` method is used to create a thread pool with 5 threads. The `submit()` method is used to submit 10 tasks to the executor, which will be executed concurrently by the 5 threads. When a thread is available, it will execute the next task in the queue, thus reusing the thread.

