To remove any previous siblings text XML nodes, you can use the following steps:

- Get the parent node of the text node you want to remove.
- Get the previous sibling node of the text node.
- While the previous sibling node is not null, remove it from the parent node.
- Remove the text node from its parent node.

Here's a sample Java code snippet that demonstrates this:

```java
import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.Element;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import org.xml.sax.InputSource;

import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder;
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import java.io.StringReader;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a sample XML document
        String xmlString = "<root><person><name>John</name><age>30</age></person><person><name>Jane</name><age>25</age></person></root>";
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        DocumentBuilder builder = factory.newDocumentBuilder();
        Document document = builder.parse(new InputSource(new StringReader(xmlString)));

        // Get the root element
        Element root = document.getDocumentElement();

        // Find all text nodes
        NodeList textNodes = root.getElementsByTagName("*");

        // Remove all previous siblings text nodes
        for (int i = textNodes.getLength() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            Node textNode = textNodes.item(i);
            if (textNode.getNodeType() == Node.TEXT_NODE) {
                Node parentNode = textNode.getParentNode();
                parentNode.removeChild(textNode);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code snippet creates a sample XML document, finds all text nodes, and removes all previous siblings text nodes.

