Here's the Java code to check a password with regex, only number and letter, length 6-10:

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String password = "A123456";
        if (checkPassword(password)) {
            System.out.println("Password is valid");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Password is not valid");
        }
    }

    public static boolean checkPassword(String password) {
        String regex = "^(?=.*[a-zA-Z])(?=.*\\d).{6,10}$";
        return Pattern.matches(regex, password);
    }
}
```

Here's what each part of the request serves based on the documentation:

- `^(?=.*[a-zA-Z])`: This part of the regex ensures that the password contains at least one letter (both uppercase and lowercase). The `(?=.*[a-zA-Z])` is a positive lookahead assertion that checks if the current position in the string is followed by at least one letter. 

- `(?=\\d)`: This part of the regex ensures that the password contains at least one digit. The `(?=\\d)` is a positive lookahead assertion that checks if the current position in the string is followed by at least one digit.

- `.{6,10}$`: This part of the regex ensures that the password has a length between 6 and 10 characters. The `.{6,10}` matches any character (including none) between 6 and 10 times, and the `$` ensures that the match must be at the end of the string.

- `Pattern.matches(regex, password)`: This method checks if the password matches the given regex pattern. It returns true if the password matches the pattern, and false otherwise.

