Here's a simple implementation of the N-D unit vector in the direction of the vector supplied:

```java
import java.lang.Math;

public class UnitVector {
    public static double[] getUnitVector(double[] vector) {
        double magnitude = Math.hypot(vector[0], vector[1]);
        if (magnitude == 0) {
            throw new ArithmeticException("Cannot compute unit vector for a zero vector");
        }
        double[] unitVector = new double[vector.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < vector.length; i++) {
            unitVector[i] = vector[i] / magnitude;
        }
        return unitVector;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[] vector = {3, 4};
        double[] unitVector = getUnitVector(vector);
        for (double value : unitVector) {
            System.out.println(value);
        }
    }
}
```

This code first computes the magnitude of the vector using `Math.hypot()`. It then normalizes the vector by dividing each component by the magnitude. The result is a unit vector in the direction of the original vector.

