3. `java.awt.image.BufferedImage.getGraphics()`: This method returns a graphics context that can be used to draw the PNG image. It serves to provide a way to draw the image on the frame, allowing you to customize its appearance.

4. `java.awt.Graphics2D.drawImage()`: This method draws an image on the graphics context, which can be used to display the PNG image. It serves to actually display the image on the frame, using the settings specified by the graphics context.

5. `java.awt.Frame.setExtendedState()`: This method sets the extended state of the frame, which can be used to make the frame undecorated and allow it to display a PNG image. It serves to remove the default window decorations, such as borders and title bars.

Here is an example of how you can use these API methods to display a PNG image on a JFrame:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import javax.imageio.ImageIO;

public class PNGDisplay {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Create a new JFrame
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("PNG Display");
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        // Create a new BufferedImage to hold the PNG image
        BufferedImage image = new BufferedImage(400, 300, BufferedImage.TYPE_INT_ARGB);

        // Load the PNG image into the BufferedImage
        File file = new File("image.png");
        ImageIO.read(file, "png");
        Graphics2D g2d = image.createGraphics();
        g2d.setRenderingHint(RenderingHints.KEY_ANTIALIASING, RenderingHints.VALUE_ANTIALIAS_ON);
        g2d.drawImage(image, 0, 0, null);

        // Create a new JLabel to hold the PNG image
        JLabel label = new JLabel(new ImageIcon(image));

        // Add the label to the frame
        frame.getContentPane().add(label);

        // Set the frame to be undecorated
        frame.setExtendedState(JFrame.MAXIMIZED_BOTH);

        // Set the shape of the frame to be the size of the image
        frame.setShape(new Rectangle(0, 0, image.getWidth(), image.getHeight()));

        // Pack the frame to ensure it is the correct size
        frame.pack();

        // Center the frame on the screen
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);

        // Make the frame visible
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
