1. `java.util.Iterator.hasNext()`: This method serves as a check to see if there are more elements to process in the stack. It allows the iterator to determine whether it should continue iterating or throw an exception.

2. `javax.swing.text.html.parser.AttributeList.getNext()`: This method is not directly applicable to the custom stack implementation. However, if we consider the concept of "next" in the context of a stack, it could be seen as a way to retrieve the next element from the stack. In this case, it would serve as a method to return the top element from the stack.

Here's a simple implementation of a custom stack in Java:

```java
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class CustomStack<T> implements Iterable<T> {

    private T[] stack;
    private int size;

    public CustomStack(int capacity) {
        stack = (T[]) new Object[capacity];
        size = 0;
    }

    public void push(T element) {
        if (size == stack.length) {
            resize();
        }
        stack[size] = element;
        size++;
    }

    public T pop() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        T element = stack[size - 1];
        stack[size - 1] = null;
        size--;
        return element;
    }

    public T peek() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return stack[size - 1];
    }

    @Override
    public Iterator<T> iterator() {
        return new StackIterator();
    }

    private void resize() {
        T[] newStack = (T[]) new Object[stack.length * 2];
        System.arraycopy(stack, 0, newStack, 0, stack.length);
        stack = newStack;
    }

    private class StackIterator implements Iterator<T> {

        private int index = 0;

        @Override
        public boolean hasNext() {
            return index < size;
        }

        @Override
        public T next() {
            if (!hasNext()) {
                throw new NoSuchElementException("Stack is empty");
            }
            return stack[index++];
        }
    }
}
```

This custom stack implementation includes methods for pushing elements onto the stack, popping elements from the stack, peeking at the top element, and iterating over the elements in the stack. The `iterator()` method returns an `Iterator` object that allows you to iterate over the elements in the stack.

