        System.out.println("Before removing last element: " + linkedList);
        linkedList.removeLast();
        System.out.println("After removing last element: " + linkedList);
    }
}
```

The `removeLast()` method serves two purposes:

- It removes the last element from the list, which is the element at the tail of the list.
- It returns the removed element, which can be useful for logging or debugging purposes.

However, note that LinkedList is not a singly linked list. It is a doubly linked list. The `removeLast()` method works for a doubly linked list, not a singly linked list. 

If you want to implement a singly linked list in Java, you can do it manually without using the LinkedList class. Here's an example:

```java
public class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    public Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

public class SinglyLinkedList {
    Node head;

    public void add(int data) {
        Node newNode = new Node(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node lastNode = head;
            while (lastNode.next != null) {
                lastNode = lastNode.next;
            }
            lastNode.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    public void removeLast() {
        if (head == null) {
            return;
        }
        if (head.next == null) {
            head = null;
        } else {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.next.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = null;
        }
    }

    public void printList() {
        Node current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " ");
            current = current.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SinglyLinkedList linkedList = new SinglyLinkedList();
        linkedList.add(1);
        linkedList.add(2);
        linkedList.add(3);
        linkedList.add(4);
        linkedList.add(5);
        System.out.println("Before removing last element: ");
        linkedList.printList();
        linkedList.removeLast();
        System.out.println("After removing last element: ");
        linkedList.printList();
    }
}
```

The `removeLast()` method in this implementation works in O(1) time complexity because it only needs to traverse the list once to find the second-to-last node, and then it can directly update the `next` pointer of the second-to-last node to null, effectively removing the last node from the list.

