To implement a queue using DelayQueue, you can use the `java.util.concurrent.DelayQueue` class, which is a thread-safe queue of delay elements. Here's a simple example of how to use it:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.DelayQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class DelayQueueExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a DelayQueue
        DelayQueue<Runnable> queue = new DelayQueue<>();

        // Add elements to the queue with a delay
        queue.add(new DelayedTask("Task 1", 1000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));
        queue.add(new DelayedTask("Task 2", 2000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));
        queue.add(new DelayedTask("Task 3", 3000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS));

        // Process the tasks in the queue
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            try {
                // Get the next task from the queue
                Runnable task = queue.take();
                task.run();
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }
    }

    private static class DelayedTask implements Runnable {
        private final String name;
        private final long delay;
        private final TimeUnit unit;

        public DelayedTask(String name, long delay, TimeUnit unit) {
            this.name = name;
            this.delay = delay;
            this.unit = unit;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            System.out.println(name + " executed");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a `DelayQueue` and add tasks to it with a delay using the `add` method. We then process the tasks in the queue using the `take` method, which blocks until a task is available. The `run` method of each task is executed when it is taken from the queue.

