Here's the Java code to calculate the natural log of the Binomial Coefficient:

```java
import java.lang.Math;

public class BinomialCoefficientLog {

    public static double binomialCoefficientLog(int n, int k) {
        // Calculate the binomial coefficient using the formula n! / (k! * (n-k)!)
        double binomialCoefficient = factorial(n) / (factorial(k) * factorial(n - k));

        // Return the natural log of the binomial coefficient
        return Math.log(binomialCoefficient);
    }

    // Helper function to calculate the factorial of a number
    private static double factorial(int number) {
        double result = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= number; i++) {
            result *= i;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10;
        int k = 3;
        double result = binomialCoefficientLog(n, k);
        System.out.println("The natural log of the binomial coefficient " + n + " choose " + k + " is: " + result);
    }
}
```

Here's an explanation of the `Math.log()` API method:

- The `Math.log()` method serves as a part of the request to calculate the natural logarithm (base e) of a double value. It returns the natural logarithm of the input value, which is the power to which the base number e must be raised to produce the given value.

