### 2. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.setConsumed(boolean)` 

This method serves to mark the event as consumed, meaning it will not be processed by the source that generated it. This can be used in conjunction with `consume()` to explicitly set the event as consumed.

### 3. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.consume(int)` 

This method serves to consume a specific type of event. By passing an event type to `consume()`, you can target specific events and prevent them from being processed.

### 4. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.consume(int, boolean)` 

This method serves to consume a specific type of event and also mark it as consumed. This can be used to target specific events and prevent them from being processed, while also explicitly setting the event as consumed.

### 5. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.setConsumed(int, boolean)` 

This method serves to mark a specific type of event as consumed. By passing an event type and a boolean value to `setConsumed()`, you can target specific events and prevent them from being processed.

### 5. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.consumeWhenFocused(boolean)` 

This method serves to consume events when the component is focused. By calling `consumeWhenFocused()` on the input event, you can prevent events from being processed when the component has focus.

### 5. `java.awt.event.InputEvent.consumeWhenFocused(boolean, boolean)` 

This method serves to consume events when the component is focused and also mark the event as consumed. This can be used to target specific events and prevent them from being processed when the component has focus, while also explicitly setting the event as consumed.

### Example Usage:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.InputEvent;
import java.awt.event.InputEvent.consume;
import java.awt.event.InputEvent.setConsumed;

public class JScrollPaneOverdraw extends JPanel {

    public JScrollPaneOverdraw() {
        setLayout(new BorderLayout());

        JScrollPane scrollPane = new JScrollPane(this);
        add(scrollPane, BorderLayout.CENTER);

        // Simulate a mouse click event
        InputEvent event = new InputEvent();
        event.setConsumed(InputEvent.MOUSE_PRESSED);
        event.consume();
    }

    @Override
    protected void paintComponent(Graphics g) {
        super.paintComponent(g);

        // Draw something on top of the JScrollPane
        g.setColor(Color.RED);
        g.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> {
            JFrame frame = new JFrame("Scroll Pane Overdraw");
            frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
            frame.add(new JScrollPaneOverdraw());
            frame.pack();
            frame.setVisible(true);
        });
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a `JPanel` and add it to a `JScrollPane`. We then simulate a mouse click event using `InputEvent` and call `consume()` to prevent the default action from being performed. Finally, we draw something on top of the `JScrollPane` in the `paintComponent()` method.

