1. `java.awt.Color.getRed()`: This method returns the red component of the color in the range 0-255 in the default sRGB space. It serves as the first part of the RGB to HSL conversion process, providing the hue component.

2. `java.awt.Color.getGreen()`: This method returns the green component of the color in the range 0-255 in the default sRGB space. It serves as the second part of the RGB to HSL conversion process, providing the saturation component.

3. `java.awt.Color.getBlue()`: This method returns the blue component of the color in the range 0-255 in the default sRGB space. It serves as the third part of the RGB to HSL conversion process, providing the luminance component.

Here's a simple Java function that uses these methods to convert an RGB color to HSL:

```java
import java.awt.Color;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int red = 255;
        int green = 0;
        int blue = 0;

        Color color = new Color(red, green, blue);
        double[] hsl = rgbToHsl(color.getRed(), color.getGreen(), color.getBlue());

        System.out.println("Hue: " + hsl[0] + ", Saturation: " + hsl[1] + ", Luminance: " + hsl[2]);
    }

    public static double[] rgbToHsl(int red, int green, int blue) {
        double r = red / 255.0;
        double g = green / 255.0;
        double b = blue / 255.0;

        double max = Math.max(r, Math.max(g, b));
        double min = Math.min(r, Math.min(g, b));

        double delta = max - min;

        double h = 0;
        if (delta != 0) {
            if (max == r) {
                h = (g - b) / delta;
            } else if (max == g) {
                h = 2 + (b - r) / delta;
            } else {
                h = 4 + (r - g) / delta;
            }
            h *= 60;
            if (h < 0) {
                h += 360;
            }
        }

        double s = 0;
        if (max != 0) {
            s = delta / max;
        }

        double l = (max + min) / 2;

        return new double[]{h, s, l};
    }
}
```

