Here's how to freeze a HashMap in Java using the `java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap()` method, along with explanations of what each part of the request serves based on its documentation:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Collections;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a HashMap
        Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put("key1", "value1");
        map.put("key2", "value2");

        // Freeze the HashMap to prevent further changes
        Map<String, String> unmodifiableMap = Collections.unmodifiableMap(map);

        // Attempting to modify the unmodifiable map will result in an UnsupportedOperationException
        try {
            unmodifiableMap.put("key3", "value3");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("Cannot modify an unmodifiable map.");
        }

        // Query operations on the unmodifiable map "read through" to the original map
        System.out.println(unmodifiableMap.get("key1"));  // Output: value1
    }
}
```

Explanation of the `java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap()` method:

- `Returns an unmodifiable view of the specified map.`: This part of the request serves to provide a read-only view of the original map, allowing modules to provide users with "read-only" access to internal maps.
- `This method allows modules to provide users with "read-only" access to internal maps.`: This part of the request serves to enable developers to create a read-only interface to a map, while still allowing them to access the original map for query operations.
- `Query operations on the returned map "read through" to the specified map, and attempts to modify the returned map, whether direct or via its collection views, result in an UnsupportedOperationException.`: This part of the request serves to ensure that any modifications made to the unmodifiable map will result in an `UnsupportedOperationException`, preventing unintended changes to the original map.
- `The returned map will be serializable if the specified map is serializable.`: This part of the request serves to ensure that the unmodifiable map is serializable if the original map is serializable, allowing it to be safely stored or transmitted.

