4. `java.util.regex.Pattern.compile()`: This method compiles a regular expression into a matcher. It serves as a way to define a URL pattern and create a matcher that can be used to find or replace URLs in a string.

5. `java.net.URI`: This class represents a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI). It serves as a way to parse a URL into its components, such as the scheme, host, path, query, and fragment.

Here's an example of how you can use these methods to parse a URL from a String in Android:

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class URLParser {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws URISyntaxException {
        String urlString = "https://www.example.com/path/to/resource?query=param#fragment";
        String pattern = "https?://[^\\s]+";

        Pattern patternObj = Pattern.compile(pattern);
        Matcher matcher = patternObj.matcher(urlString);

        while (matcher.find()) {
            System.out.println("URL: " + matcher.group());
